UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 11: Dance elements and technique', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A useful description names dance elements and the effect those choices have on an audience. Elements from this unit include pathway, duration, level, direction, dynamic, relationship, motif, phrase, focus and stillness. A strong description says what could be seen and what that choice helps a viewer understand or feel. \"It was good\" does not name an element, so another person cannot check it. Describe a moment you watched, remembered or planned on paper.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA classmate only writes that the dance was nice and that the person moved a lot. How would you rewrite a sharp, high, quick reach that then freezes, using three element words and one audience effect?\n\nFEEDBACK\nName the high level, the quick time and the sharp stop, then say the freeze lets the audience catch the call. \"Nice\" and \"moved a lot\" hide which elements did the work. Three element names plus one effect are enough for a checkable description. You are describing, not scoring talent.\n\nWATCH FOR\nWords such as amazing or boring are treated as better description than the names of dance elements. Talent words skip the evidence. An audience effect has to be tied to a visible choice, such as a low level or a sharp stop. Otherwise the comment cannot be checked or improved.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nDescribe a new moment you invent, not the empty-chair hold and not the high reach. Use at least four element words and explain one audience effect for each of two choices. Write in full sentences a viewer could check against the plan.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Dance","learning_intention":"A useful description names dance elements and the effect those choices have on an audience. Elements from this unit include pathway, duration, level, direction, dynamic, relationship, motif, phrase, focus and stillness. A strong description says what could be seen and what that choice helps a viewer understand or feel. \"It was good\" does not name an element, so another person cannot check it. Describe a moment you watched, remembered or planned on paper.","lesson_number":11,"lesson_role":"Learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["DA4-PER-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Describe a new moment you invent, not the empty-chair hold and not the high reach. Use at least four element words and explain one audience effect for each of two choices. Write in full sentences a viewer could check aga Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using element vocabulary on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Words such as amazing or boring are treated as better description than the names of dance elements.. Correction: Name the high level, the quick time and the sharp stop, then say the freeze lets the audience catch the call.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Words such as amazing or boring are treated as better description than the names of dance elements. Talent words skip the evidence. An audience effect has to be tied to a visible choice, such as a low level or a sharp stop. Otherwise the comment cannot be checked or improved. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating element vocabulary on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with element vocabulary, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about element vocabulary with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for element vocabulary. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for element vocabulary. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name the high level, the quick time and the sharp stop, then say the freeze lets the audience catch the call","Words such as amazing or boring are treated as better description than the names of dance elements","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work"],"question":"A classmate only writes that the dance was nice and that the person moved a lot. How would you rewrite a sharp, high, quick reach that then freezes, using three element words and one audience effect?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for element vocabulary; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Words such as amazing or boring are treated as better description than the names of dance elements","A useful description names dance elements and the effect those choices have on an audience","Name the high level, the quick time and the sharp stop, then say the freeze lets the audience catch the call","Careful use of audience effect with clear evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with element vocabulary?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named audience effect, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name audience effect only, without linking it to element vocabulary","Apply element vocabulary to new material and name the audience effect used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Words such as amazing or boring are treated as better description than the names of dance elements"],"question":"Which response best applies element vocabulary with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on element vocabulary. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Words such as amazing or boring are treated as better description than the names of dance elements","Name the high level, the quick time and the sharp stop, then say the freeze lets the audience catch the call"],"question":"Which response best evaluates elements, techniques and audience effect when analysing element vocabulary?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Words such as amazing or boring are treated as better description than the names of dance elements","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for element vocabulary","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about element vocabulary?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (A useful description names dance elements and the effect those choices) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Words such as amazing or boring are treated as better description than the names of dance elements","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","A dancer sinks on a slow sustained path to a low level and holds stillness while looking at an empty chair"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about element vocabulary?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete"],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for element vocabulary?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Words such as amazing or boring are treated as better description than the names of dance elements","Name audience effect only, with no linked example","Describe a new moment you invent, not the empty-chair hold and not the high reach","Retype the worked example with no new example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies element vocabulary to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about element vocabulary with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating element vocabulary on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using element vocabulary on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for element vocabulary. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with element vocabulary, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Words such as amazing or boring are treated as better description than the names of dance elements.. Correction: Name the high level, the quick time and the sharp stop, then say the freeze lets the audience catch the call.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-dance-y9-w06-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 4 alignment to Dance 7–10 (2023) outcome DA4-PER-01. Content-point mapping pending. Not NESA verified. Movement is optional, pain-free, and adult-supervised. Written plans are acceptable evidence.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise element vocabulary.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Dance","success_criteria":["I can use element vocabulary with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify audience effect and explain why it matters.","I can practise a dance description independently on a new prompt.","I can notice evidence needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"A useful description names dance elements and the effect those choices have on an audience.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","A useful description names dance elements and the effect those choices have on an audience"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about element vocabulary?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA dancer sinks on a slow sustained path to a low level and holds stillness while looking at an empty chair. Element vocabulary: low level, slow duration, sustained dynamic, focus and ending stillness. Audience effect: the slowness and the held look make waiting feel unfinished, so viewers stay with the chair too. The note never stops at \"it was emotional\".\n\nTRY FIRST\nA classmate only writes that the dance was nice and that the person moved a lot. How would you rewrite a sharp, high, quick reach that then freezes, using three element words and one audience effect?\n\nFEEDBACK\nName the high level, the quick time and the sharp stop, then say the freeze lets the audience catch the call. \"Nice\" and \"moved a lot\" hide which elements did the work. Three element names plus one effect are enough for a checkable description. You are describing, not scoring talent.","explain":"A useful description names dance elements and the effect those choices have on an audience. Elements from this unit include pathway, duration, level, direction, dynamic, relationship, motif, phrase, focus and stillness. A strong description says what could be seen and what that choice helps a viewer understand or feel. \"It was good\" does not name an element, so another person cannot check it. Describe a moment you watched, remembered or planned on paper.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Words such as amazing or boring are treated as better description than the names of dance elements. Talent words skip the evidence. An audience effect has to be tied to a visible choice, such as a low level or a sharp stop. Otherwise the comment cannot be checked or improved. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Two panels contrast a talent word with no element and a description linking slow and low movement to waiting.","caption":"Name an element and its effect on the audience. A talent word alone cannot be checked.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Talent judgement</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Amazing, but no element</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Element and effect</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Slow and low makes us wait</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: A dancer sinks on a slow sustained path to a low level and holds stillness while looking at an empty chair. Element voca Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: A dancer sinks on a slow sustained path to a low level and holds stillness while looking at an empty chair. Element voca Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about element vocabulary?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA dancer sinks on a slow sustained path to a low level and holds stillness while looking at an empty chair. Element vocabulary: low level, slow duration, sustained dynamic, focus and ending stillness. Audience effect: the slowness and the held look make waiting feel unfinished, so viewers stay with the chair too. The note never stops at \"it was emotional\". Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Two panels contrast a vague praise comment with a description of a high, quick, sharp reach that is then held.","caption":"A checkable description names elements such as level, time and dynamic, then says what the audience can read.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Vague comment</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Nice, and they moved a lot</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Checkable comment</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">High, quick, sharp, then held</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about element vocabulary?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nWords such as amazing or boring are treated as better description than the names of dance elements. Talent words skip the evidence. An audience effect has to be tied to a visible choice, such as a low level or a sharp stop. Otherwise the comment cannot be checked or improved. Here is a more helpful example: A dancer sinks on a slow sustained path to a low level and holds stillness while looking at an empty chair. Element vocabulary: low level, slow duration, sustained dynamic, focus and ending stillness. Audience effect: the slowness and the held look make waiting feel unfinished, so viewers stay with the chair too. The note never stops at \"it was emotional\".\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nName the high level, the quick time and the sharp stop, then say the freeze lets the audience catch the call. \"Nice\" and \"moved a lot\" hide which elements did the work. Three element names plus one effect are enough for a checkable description. You are describing, not scoring talent.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Words such as amazing or boring are treated as better description than the names of dance elements. Talent words skip the evidence. An audience effect has to be tied to a visible choice, such as a low level or a sharp stop. Otherwise the comment cannot be checked or improved. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about element vocabulary?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA useful description names dance elements and the effect those choices have on an audience. Elements from this unit include pathway, duration, level, direction, dynamic, relationship, motif, phrase, focus and stillness. A strong description says what could be seen and what that choice helps a viewer understand or feel. \"It was good\" does not name an element, so another person cannot check it. Describe a moment you watched, remembered or planned on paper. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 11: Dance elements and technique","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Dance elements and technique","week":9,"week_in_unit":6,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nA dancer sinks on a slow sustained path to a low level and holds stillness while looking at an empty chair. Element vocabulary: low level, slow duration, sustained dynamic, focus and ending stillness. Audience effect: the slowness and the held look make waiting feel unfinished, so viewers stay with the chair too. The note never stops at \"it was emotional\".\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Two panels contrast a talent word with no element and a description linking slow and low movement to waiting.","caption":"Name an element and its effect on the audience. A talent word alone cannot be checked.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Talent judgement</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Amazing, but no element</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Element and effect</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Slow and low makes us wait</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Two panels contrast a vague praise comment with a description of a high, quick, sharp reach that is then held.","caption":"A checkable description names elements such as level, time and dynamic, then says what the audience can read.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Vague comment</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Nice, and they moved a lot</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Checkable comment</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">High, quick, sharp, then held</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s5-dance-y9-w06-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
